   "output": "You are comparing two drafts written from the same brief. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse; judge only the text below.\n\nBrief: Write the docs-site page for the `term-weave` skill with the sections What it does, When to use it, How it works, and What you get.\n\n<<<DRAFT A\n# term-weave\n\n## What it does\n\n`term-weave` drafts a glossary of project terms from documents in a supplied folder. Every drafted definition links to the passage that supports it.\n\n## When to use it\n\nUse it when project vocabulary is scattered across documents or newcomers encounter unexplained terms.\n\n## How it works\n\nRun the skill manually:\n\n```text\n/term-weave <source-folder>\n```\n\nIt reads Markdown and plain-text documents and ignores other file types.\n\nIf the folder is missing or contains no readable supported documents, it stops and explains the problem without drafting.\n\nThe skill does not invent definitions. It lists terms without a supporting explanation under **Unresolved terms**.\n\nWhen sources give conflicting definitions, it preserves both in one entry, links each source, and marks the entry **Conflict**.\n\n## What you get\n\nThe skill writes `GLOSSARY.md` in the working directory. Defined entries appear alphabetically, followed by **Unresolved terms**.\n\nThe source documents remain unchanged.\nDRAFT A>>>\n\n<<<DRAFT B\n## What it does\n\n`term-weave` drafts a glossary of project terms from documents in a folder you supply.\n\n## When to use it\n\nUse it when project vocabulary is scattered across documents or newcomers encounter unexplained terms.\n\n## How it works\n\nRun the skill manually with `/term-weave <source-folder>`.\n\nFor example, if your documents are in `./project-docs`, run:\n\n```text\n/term-weave ./project-docs\n```\n\nIt reads Markdown and plain-text documents and ignores other file types. It never changes the source documents.\n\nIf the folder is missing or contains no readable supported documents, it stops and explains the problem without drafting.\n\n## What you get\n\nThe skill writes `GLOSSARY.md` in the working directory, with glossary entries sorted alphabetically.\n\nEvery drafted definition includes a link to the passage that supports it. The skill does not invent definitions. Terms without a supporting explanation appear under **Unresolved terms**, after the defined entries.\n\nIf sources give conflicting definitions, the skill preserves both definitions in one entry, links each source, and marks the entry **Conflict**.\nDRAFT B>>>\n\nAnswer each question with \"A\", \"B\", or \"SAME\":\n- \"reader\": Could a first-time reader do what they came to do?\n- \"careful\": Does it read like a careful person wrote it?\n- \"overall\": Which draft is better overall?\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"reader\": \"A\", \"careful\": \"A\", \"overall\": \"A\", \"reason\": \"<one sentence>\"}",
